# Infohole Shroud

The shroud page belongs after causal order, cones, incidence, and curvature. An
infohole is not admitted here as a primitive physical object. In this phase it
is a no-readout region generated by causal settling and a chosen readout region.

The claim-site carried here is:

* claim-site: finite infohole shroud and shroud-sourced effect object;
* phase: relational spacetime;
* generating sites: settled causal order, causal regions, future-closed regions,
  readout reachability, shroud adjacency, and shroud-supported effects;
* downstream uses: infohole frontier diagnostics, dense-matter boundary
  comparisons, shroud/cohomology questions, and keratinous-disc frontier pages;
* Lean carrier: `Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole`;
* Blueprint/proof-tree nodes: `thm:infohole-region`,
  `thm:infohole-future-closed`, and `thm:infohole-shroud`; richer
  descent-and-effects nodes remain frontier;
* provenance: the infohole frontier asks for a finite no-readout region before
  any geometric or color theorem is made;
* familiar comparison: event horizon as a later physical reading of a no-readout
  shroud;
* reader closure: read "shroud" here as finite no-readout boundary, not as a
  smooth null surface;
* frontier status: the causal `Infohole` carrier is settled here with a
  shroud-supported effect relation. Descent obstruction, richer persistence,
  entropy, and any eventual coloring theorem remain frontier.

The governing sentence is:

> The finite infohole region is the complement of causal readout.

## Readout And No Readout

The readout predicate is:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole.ReachesReadout}

The no-readout region is:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole.InfoholeRegion}

and the exterior is its readout side:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole.ReadoutExterior}

The definition is deliberately finite. It needs a causal set and a region
playing readout. It does not need a smooth manifold, metric tensor, conformal
boundary, or color theorem.

## Closure Laws

The main law is future closure:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole.infohole_future_closed}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E) (readout : Region E) :
    FutureClosedRegion C (InfoholeRegion C readout) :=
  infohole_future_closed C readout
```

The readout exterior is past-closed:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole.readoutExterior_past_closed}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E) (readout : Region E)
    {x y : E}
    (hxy : CausalLe C x y)
    (hy : ReadoutExterior C readout y) :
    ReadoutExterior C readout x :=
  readoutExterior_past_closed C readout hxy hy
```

Interior events do not causally precede exterior events:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole.infohole_no_future_to_exterior}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E) (readout : Region E)
    {x y : E}
    (hin : InfoholeRegion C readout x)
    (hout : ReadoutExterior C readout y) :
    Not (CausalLe C x y) :=
  infohole_no_future_to_exterior C readout hin hout
```

These are the lawful claims. No-readout propagates to the future; readout
reachability propagates to the past. That is the causal-order skeleton of the
shroud.

## Boundary

The finite adjacency predicate is:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole.CausalAdjacent}

The shroud is:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole.Shroud}

A shroud point lies in the infohole region and has an exterior neighbor:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole.shroud_in_infohole}

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole.shroud_has_exterior_neighbour}

The exterior neighbor is forced to precede the shroud event:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole.shroud_has_exterior_predecessor}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E) (readout : Region E)
    {x : E}
    (h : Shroud C readout x) :
    InfoholeRegion C readout x :=
  shroud_in_infohole C readout h

example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E) (readout : Region E)
    {x : E}
    (h : Shroud C readout x) :
    Not (ReadoutExterior C readout x) :=
  shroud_not_exterior C readout h
```

The familiar word "horizon" is now only a later physical reading of these
finite laws. The theorem does not say that the shroud is null, smooth, area-bearing,
thermodynamic, semiclassical, or black-colored. Those are later frontier
promotions.

